{"items":[{"id":"cap-v2-ordering_and_selection-javascript-generation-01","problem":"Implement a stateful ranked picker in one JavaScript source file.

Export exactly this CommonJS API:

module.exports = { createRankedPicker };

`createRankedPicker()` returns an object with three methods:

- `add(id, score)`: adds an entry. Inputs are valid strings and finite numbers. Repeated IDs are allowed and represent separate entries.
- `take(count)`: removes and returns up to `count` highest-ranked entries as `{ id, score }` objects.
- `list()`: returns all current entries as `{ id, score }` objects without removing them.

Entries rank by descending `score`. Equal scores retain their insertion order. Every picker must own independent entries and insertion-order state, so operations on one picker cannot affect another picker.

Use only the JavaScript standard library. Do not access the filesystem, network, clock, or randomness, and do not use external packages or frameworks.","reference_code":"function createRankedPicker() {
  const entries = [];
  let sequence = 0;

  function ranked() {
    return entries.slice().sort((a, b) => {
      if (a.score !== b.score) return b.score - a.score;
      return a.sequence - b.sequence;
    });
  }

  return {
    add(id, score) {
      entries.push({ id, score, sequence: sequence++ });
    },

    take(count) {
      const chosen = ranked().slice(0, count);
      const chosenSequences = new Set(chosen.map((entry) => entry.sequence));

      for (let index = entries.length - 1; index >= 0; index -= 1) {
        if (chosenSequences.has(entries[index].sequence)) {
          entries.splice(index, 1);
        }
      }

      return chosen.map(({ id, score }) => ({ id, score }));
    },

    list() {
      return ranked().map(({ id, score }) => ({ id, score }));
    }
  };
}

module.exports = { createRankedPicker };
